The reality is a lot of this
data never gets looked at.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.950
Because most of the control
systems in place today,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.920
they still look at
a few variables,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.530
and that's how you
drive your operations.

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:45.160
With machine learning, what we
do is we go through a series

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.250
of data exploration, what
we call as data exploration

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.310
and analysis, which takes a
look at which of all these--

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:55.940
and in this case, we looked
at about 50 plus variables--

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:01.060
which of these have the direct
input to what is the output

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:02.440
that we are trying to achieve.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:05.350
And in this case, the key output
that we are trying to achieve

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.080
are two things.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.620
One is ensure that
we can figure out

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.230
when the filter will
have to be replaced,

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.960
so that the quality
of beer is maintained.

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:17.110
In this situation there is
another output that we also

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:20.450
have to watch out for, which
is, as a part of the filter

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.700
operation, there is something
called as a differential

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.580
of pressure that builds up.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:28.550
And if the pressure exceeds
a certain threshold,

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.370
the filter automatically
goes into what

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:32.480
we call as a restart model.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.780
So there are two things
that we look for,

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:38.410
which is the filter, what is
the differential pressure, as

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.910
an output measure,
or a variable.

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:44.140
And the second is
what is the prediction

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.060
for it going beyond the level.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.560
And the second is the
measure of quality,

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.150
which is what in the beer
world is called stability.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:55.120
The way this is done typically
today is a beermaster.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.970
It's part art and part science.

00:13:57.970 --> 00:13:59.800
So usually it's
the beermaster who

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.470
makes the guess to say,
OK, I think the filter that

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:03.770
has to be replaced.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:07.930
So what we really enabled
is, take that decision making

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:13.150
from a human being and replicate
it in a machine learning brain.

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:16.480
And the way we did that
is what I just explained.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:18.040
Take a look at the
input, figure out

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.500
what are the key
contributors to the output,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:23.890
go through data
exploration, data analysis.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:25.838
As a lot of who
have done machine

00:14:25.838 --> 00:14:27.880
learning-- if you are in
the audience, will know,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:31.600
80% of the time is all about
making sure that we actually

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:32.800
clean the data.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:35.350
And then the remaining
time is making sure

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.710
that you can use that
to build a model.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:42.300
So this is a summary
of how we did it.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:43.675
And I'll go through
more details.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.970
So the key thing,
as in any machine

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:53.120
learning, for a base
solution, is making sure

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:54.110
that you have the data.

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:55.550
I already said that there was--

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.440
in the production process,
or in a supply chain,

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.500
there's a lot of data that's
already been collected.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:02.750
This just gives us schematic.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.740
The idea here is to visually
show you what I just mentioned.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:10.490
The key thresholds that we are
trying to predict, or control,

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:13.340
is the differential
pressure and the turbidity.

00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:16.310
And what enabled
us to achieve this

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:18.410
is the fact that we
had a data collection

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.790
system, which was already at--

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:25.370
I mean, it was in place
at New Jersey brewery,

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:27.470
and as well as in
other breweries,

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:31.280
which collected the data
in 10 seconds interval.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.970
And that data was available,
not only did they collect it,

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.930
it was actually available
in our database.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:38.270
It was a Sybase database.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:39.710
We took that data
and we loaded it

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:42.140
on the Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.530
And just to show you
this is a simpler--

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:48.290
in the interest of making
sure that we are maintaining

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:51.210
the confidentiality of
this competitive solution,

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.930
we're not going to show you
everything about the solution,

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.750
but I'm going to give you
enough information on how

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:59.840
a similar solution can be built.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:01.490
This is a simple
architecture that we

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:04.310
used during the proof of concept
and the production pilot.

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:06.520
And I'll talk to
what advances we

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:09.830
are going to do next
in an example solution

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:13.562
that I will show, which
basically I showed you

00:16:13.562 --> 00:16:15.020
where the data was
coming from-- we

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:16.700
loaded it into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:20.040
This is a result. Think
about it as just like a box.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.570
You can store many
types of data on this.

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:25.040
We took that and--

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.190
for those of you who have done
this, they've used Data Lab.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:32.600
Our team uses Data Lab,
which essentially enables

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.020
you to go through the process
of data cleaning, data

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:38.480
analysis, data
exploration, so that you

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.340
can select the algorithm.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:46.220
You can actually then make sure
that you review the results.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:50.000
After you look at the algorithm,
you feel that it is good,

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:52.280
you can then go
through the steps

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.100
of what we call as training
the model using data.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:56.510
So the training is--

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.180
in layman's terms--
think about it, right?

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:02.390
If you have a human being
that learns, the way we learn

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.099
is through a series
of experiences

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:09.230
that we get from the time
we are born until adulthood.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.780
Input data is nothing
but experiences

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:14.930
that you feed in a
machine learning model,

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:19.000
so that it can learn, these
are the outputs, this is what--

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.480
sorry-- these are
the inputs, this

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.109
was the output that happened.

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:23.240
So it builds that learning.

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:25.970
That's the process that we do,
what is called as training.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.980
And then to verify that
the learning is accurate,

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.270
we do evaluation.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.960
So all of that, those are
typical machine learning steps

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.700
that you might
have heard already.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:36.950
We did that using Data Lab.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:41.450
We use TensorFlow, a deep
learning neural network.

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:44.810
We used a combinational model
here, a classification model

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:49.940
to make sure that we can
predict when the filter was not

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:50.640
good enough.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.000
And we also had to do some
time series element to it,

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.980
because since you
are predicting,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:58.800
you need it to do the prediction
as to-- that is a time series

00:17:58.800 --> 00:17:59.300
problem.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:05.120
So we used a combinational model
and we loaded the process data

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.490
for reference in BigQuery.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.440
In this scenario,
it was enough for us

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:13.070
to give the indicator
to the production

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:17.080
people on a weekly basis, so
that they could monitor what

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:20.990
were the elements that they
had to review and change

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:22.570
to maintain--

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:26.840
making sure that the filter
was-- the life of the filter

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:29.360
was known, and then extended.

00:18:32.618 --> 00:18:37.170
So the key thing--

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:42.830
this is the key thing that I
want everybody to understand.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.460
Most of the times, when human
beings look at a process,

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.130
and even with regular
statistical base process

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:52.700
control, you only are
monitoring certain elements,

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.670
and those, whatever you
monitor is what you get.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:59.360
Using machine learning,
there are three key learnings

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.000
that were completely
counterintuitive that

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.900
came about in the joint work
that we did with AB InBev.

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:08.750
One was-- the traditional
approach was-- the thought

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.230
process was bodyfeed being
one of the-- bodyfeed

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:14.810
is the term that is
used in the industry

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:19.400
for the measure of additive
that is added to the filter

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.340
to filter the beer.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.020
The thought process was,
if we add that slowly,

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:30.740
because it's a costly
material, it will be better.

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.530
Guess what, the data showed,
and the machine learning model

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:36.740
showed that you, actually,
if you fed the bodyfeed

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.900
at a higher rate
in the beginning,

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:42.590
just for a shorter period of
time, that was more helpful.

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:46.580
This was something that even
the production brewmaster said,

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:49.190
wow, I would have
never figured this out.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:51.640
The second thing was
the fact that, OK,

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.970
you have to avoid fluctuations.

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.810
So they were trying to fluctuate
the bodyfeed in the interest

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:59.690
of, again, trying to save.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:03.110
It actually was countering
to do, and the data showed--

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:04.100
and it was brewing--

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:06.080
I'll show you the
results shortly--

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.080
that keeping the fluctuations
less and keeping it

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:13.460
steady state was actually
more helpful to prolonging

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:15.530
the life of the filter
as well as maintaining

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.480
the quality of the filter.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:21.470
Thirdly, making sure
that your bodyfeed

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:25.790
was optimized to the lowest
level at 30 pounds per hour

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.050
actually helped.

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.570
And they were not keeping
it at 30 pounds per hour.

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:32.370
They were actually keeping
it at a higher level.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.790
So, again, now, that
was a direct correlation

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:36.090
to the cost savings.

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.420
So net-net is-- these
are some examples

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:39.980
that I wanted to point out.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:41.840
The net-net is-- these
are the results that

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:45.290
actually were observed
from the production pilot.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:47.510
We did the production
pilot on two lines

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:49.810
in the New Jersey brewery.

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:53.980
In the one line, we got an
improvement of about 44%.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:57.100
And this is comparing
the amount of runs

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.290
when you said start
and stop of the filter

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:03.970
before we did that change
and after we did the change.

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:06.760
In the second line,
we actually saw

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:11.290
that the production
improvement was much more

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:12.880
than the first line.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:15.280
And the differences
we attributed

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:17.440
was because they were--
on the second line

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:21.100
there were more opportunities
where the production

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:23.410
people were not following
a few standards,

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:25.840
so that accounted
for the distribution.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:29.050
In any case, a 44%
improvement was way more

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:32.270
than what we expected
that we started with.

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:36.250
So as a result of
this now, we have--

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:40.930
in fact, Adam, who is not
here, just between him and us,

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:44.260
we now have finalized
the other locations

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.390
that we are going to
deploy this solution to,

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:48.310
now that the production
results are out

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.800
and it looks like it
is a good thing to do.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:58.000
And so what I want to talk about
next is a quick recap of what

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:03.970
the solution was, which,
again, the idea is to make sure

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:07.810
that you take data,
you process the data,

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:12.010
you select the right
inputs, know the outputs

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.740
that you are pursuing,
and making sure

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:19.070
that you do the model, not only
do you do a proof of concept

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:20.540
but then you do a
production pilot.

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.240
The learnings from
the production pilot,

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:25.090
as we observed,
were huge in terms

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:28.000
of making sure that
now we understand

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.010
how we will deploy it
in multiple breweries,

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:32.500
as an example.

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:37.660
So like I said, I cannot
show the actual solutions.

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:40.550
It's a competitive
advantage to my customer.

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:43.690
What I'm going to do
now is show a solution--

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:45.190
and by the way,
this solution, we

